    "case_id": "302",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s presented with a 1-year history of a pruritic, dark red plaque on his right upper eyelid. No remarkable medical history was noted.\n\nA 4 × 2-cm erythematous, violaceous, indurated, scaly plaque with some papules on his right upper eyelid.\n\n- Laboratory Tests: Routine blood tests and biochemistry analysis were within normal limits.- Histopathologic Examination:  - Revealed perifollicular lymphocytic infiltrates around cystically dilated follicular infundibula  - Exocytosis of atypical lymphocytes into the epithelium  - Admixed plasma cells, eosinophils, and multinucleate giant cells  - Alcian blue staining showed focal mild follicular mucin deposition- Immunohistochemical Analysis:  - Lymphocytic infiltrates positive for CD3, CD4, CD5, and CD7  - Negative for CD8, CD20, CD30, CD79a, and CD56  - CD4 to CD8 cells ratio within hair follicles > 7:1  - Monoclonal T-cell gene rearrangement was demonstrated- Images:  Image A Title: Clinical presentation of right upper eyelid  Image A Description: 4 × 2-cm erythematous, violaceous, indurated, scaly plaque with papules    Image B Title: Lesional specimen  Image B Description: Histopathologic examination at original magnification ×50    Image C Title: High-power magnification of histopathologic examination  Image C Description: Shows atypical lymphocytes    Image D Title: Immunohistochemical staining  Image D Description: Shows lymphocytic infiltrates predominantly positive for CD4",
